    id: "f0cee96600056bd4504a",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    title: "Nursery owner in China jailed for life after hundreds of children poisoned",
    summary: "She and five staff members were found to have used the inedible paint in meals to make them look more appealing.",
    preview: "A nursery owner and five members of her staff have been jailed after they were found to have poisoned more than 200 children with lead-tainted food. Li Huifang, who operated the Heshi Peixin Kindergarten in Tianshui, in the Gansu province, was sentenced to life in prison for producing and selling toxic and harmful food, according to Chinese state media. In July 2025, 247 pupils and staff at the nursery were found to have abnormally high blood lead levels after children began falling ill.",
    body: "A nursery owner and five members of her staff have been jailed after they were found to have poisoned more than 200 children with lead-tainted food.\n\nLi Huifang, who operated the Heshi Peixin Kindergarten in Tianshui, in the Gansu province, was sentenced to life in prison for producing and selling toxic and harmful food, according to Chinese state media.\n\nIn July 2025, 247 pupils and staff at the nursery were found to have abnormally high blood lead levels after children began falling ill.\n\nA report into the incident found that chefs at Heshi Peixin had used inedible paint to \"enhance the look\" of its meals to attract more students - despite the paint being being clearly marked as such.\n\nIn addition to the sentence, the Lanzhou Intermediate People's Court also ordered the confiscation of 500,000 yuan (£56,200; $74,700) of Li's personal assets, state broadcaster CCTV reported.\n\nNursery executive principal Zhi Jinglin was sentenced to 15 years in prison and fined 300,00 yuan.\n\nFour other members of staff who worked in the nursery's kitchen received sentences of between six to eight years, according to state media.\n\nThe court found the four had been instructed by Li and Zhi to add toxic and harmful ingredients to food served to children and staff.\n\nFood samples were later found to contain lead 2,000 times in excess of China's national safety limit.\n\nThe investigation into the poisoning began after 235 children from Heshi Peixin nursery had to be treated for lead poisoning after eating steamed red date cakes and sausage corn buns.\n\nInvestigators found that the red date cake and the corn sausage rolls had lead levels of 1052mg/kg and 1340mg/kg respectively, both of which far exceed the national food safety standard limit of 0.5mg/kg.\n\nLead poisoning is \"particularly harmful to young children\" and can \"severely damage the brain and central nervous system\", according to the World Health Organization.\n\nOfficials later found that dozens of provincial officials and hospital staff attempted to cover up the incident by tampering with blood tests and accepting bribes from a nursery investor.\n\nChina has since published a set of national guidelines for the provision of meals on campuses. Among the measures is the requirement for every new batch of food to be tested, and for flour, rice and cooking oil to be purchased from centralised procurement points only.",
